         still land on the training plane. It stops at y=4, where the water
         starts, and runs to the horizon the other way (x -20..30, y -25..4),
         so the checkerboard never shows at the edge of a picture. -->
    <body name="dock_quay" pos="5 -10.5 0">
      <geom name="dock_quay_slab" type="box" size="25 14.5 0.006" pos="0 0 -0.004"
            material="quay" contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="2"/>
    </body>

    <!-- The harbour basin on the +y side. High tide: the water sits level
         with the quay, the same 2 mm sheet as the slab, because the training
         floor is an infinite plane at z=0 and anything drawn below it is
         hidden. Visual only; the robot never gets there, the kerb stops it
         first. The wall is the quay's coping, a dark strip along the edge
         that separates concrete from water in the picture. -->
    <body name="dock_water" pos="5 29 0">
      <geom name="dock_water_surface" type="box" size="40 25 0.006" pos="0 0 -0.004"
            material="water" contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="2"/>
